## Option A vs Option B Comparison Analysis

What Option B Measures vs Option A:

| Aspect           | Option A (Userspace)        | Option B (Kernel-Side)      |
|------------------|----------------------------|----------------------------|
| Timing location  | Around syscall() call      | Inside syscall body        |
| Includes         | Full round-trip cost       | Only kernel execution      |
| Precision        | Limited by userspace timing| High-precision ktime       |
| Overhead         | Measures timing overhead   | Excludes transition cost   |
| Variance         | Shows total variance       | Shows kernel-only variance |

### Key Observations:

1. **Timing Difference**: Option A shows ~600-800ns per call, Option B shows ~60-80ns per call
   - The difference (~540-720ns) represents userspace-to-kernel transition overhead
   - This includes syscall entry/exit, context switching, and userspace timing overhead

2. **Variance Analysis**: 
   - Option A: Shows total system variance (kernel + transition + timing)
   - Option B: Shows only kernel execution variance with min/max tracking
   - Kernel-side measurements show much lower and more consistent timing

3. **Performance Baseline**: 
   - Pure kernel syscall execution: ~60-80ns
   - Full syscall round-trip cost: ~600-800ns
   - Transition overhead ratio: ~87-90% of total syscall cost